isable-tex-rendering">Rebuttal:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value m=
arkdown-rendered"><p><strong>W1:</strong> We will supplement the related wo=
rk with existing studies on the generalization analysis of SGD with weight =
decay, including but not limited to the following references:</p>
<ul>
<li>[1] Weight decay with tailored Adam on scale-invariant weights for bett=
er generalization. (Jia et al., 2022)</li>
<li>[2] Towards Better Generalization: Weight Decay Induces Low-rank Bias f=
or Neural Networks. (Chen et al., 2024)</li>
<li>[3] Three mechanisms of weight decay regularization. (Zhang et al., 201=
8)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>W2: Contributions of This Paper</strong> We summarize our contri=
butions in the Introduction and "Answer to Question" in sections 4 and 5.</=
p>
<p><strong>Scope of the Conclusions</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Although we empirically validate the relationship between hyperparamete=
rs under general settings, our theoretical construction of the equivalence =
and the theoretical analysis are both conducted under convex assumptions. <=
/li>
<li>Our framework applies to regularization methods for which a bound can b=
e established via stability analysis.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>We present the SGD-WD's bound for two main reasons:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>To investigate how weight decay tightens the generalization bound of SG=
D within the stability analysis, which provides the foundation for establis=
hing the hyperparameter scaling rule.</li>
<li>To derive an analytical relationship between hyperparameters through th=
e regularization equivalence hypothesis based on this generalization bound.=
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>W3:</strong> We will further summarize and highlight the key tak=
eaways in the paper to improve clarity. In this work, we adopt a problem-or=
iented writing structure:</p>
<ul>
<li>We begin by posing questions: e.g., (Question 1), how does weight decay=
 affect the generalization performance of algorithms? (Question 2), how doe=
s weight decay influence hyperparameters? </li>
<li>We then answer these questions: for Q1, we establish generalization bou=
nds via stability analysis (Section 4); for Q2, we derive analytical expres=
sions between hyperparameters via the regularization equivalence hypothesis=
 (Section 5).</li>
<li>We conclude by summarizing the answers: for Q1, weight decay improves g=
eneralization performance;(Answer to Q1)  for Q2, weight decay scales inver=
sely with the learning rate and proportionally with batch size.(Answer to Q=
2)</li>
</ul>
