"><ol>
<li><p>Thank you for providing the intuition behind the regularization equi=
valence hypothesis. We find that, in its current form, it remains quite abs=
tract and is not supported by direct evidence. Providing a simple toy examp=
le where this hypothesis can be explicitly verified would significantly str=
engthen the paper.</p>
<p> As it stands, the argument risks appearing circular=E2=80=94i.e., the h=
ypothesis is justified primarily because it leads to empirically correct sc=
aling laws such as <mjx-container class=3D"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0" jax=
=3D"CHTML" tabindex=3D"0" ctxtmenu_counter=3D"308" style=3D"font-size: 121.=
3%; position: relative;"><mjx-math class=3D"MJX-TEX" aria-hidden=3D"true"><=
mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-i"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c1D706 TEX-I"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><=
mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-i"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c1D702 TEX-I"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><=
/mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=3D"on" display=3D"inline"><math x=
mlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>=CE=BB</mi><mi>=CE=B7</mi><=
/math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container>=3Dconst. Moreover, the intuition=
 does not address the speed of convergence. In practice, especially in LLM =
pre-training, training might operate in an early regime where the optimum i=
s not reached, and convergence speed is crucial. Yet the condition <mjx-con=
tainer class=3D"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0" jax=3D"CHTML" tabindex=3D"0" c=
txtmenu_counter=3D"309" style=3D"font-size: 121.3%; position: relative;"><m=
jx-math class=3D"MJX-TEX" aria-hidden=3D"true"><mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-i"><mjx=
-c class=3D"mjx-c1D706 TEX-I"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-i"><mjx=
-c class=3D"mjx-c1D702 TEX-I"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=3D"mjx-n" spac=
e=3D"4"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c3D"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-math><mjx-assistive=
-mml unselectable=3D"on" display=3D"inline"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.or=
g/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>=CE=BB</mi><mi>=CE=B7</mi><mo>=3D</mo></math></mjx-=
assistive-mml></mjx-container>const appears to hold even in this regime (e.=
g., Wang &amp; Aitchison, 2025, Nano-GPT results), which raises further que=
stions about the underlying mechanism.</p>
<p> To summarize, a more sophisticated justification for such a hypothesis =
is necessary in this work.</p>
<p> I would like to raise the following question: weight decay introduces a=
 tunable parameter <mjx-container class=3D"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0" jax=
=3D"CHTML" tabindex=3D"0" ctxtmenu_counter=3D"310" style=3D"font-size: 121.=
3%; position: relative;"><mjx-math class=3D"MJX-TEX" aria-hidden=3D"true"><=
mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-i"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c1D706 TEX-I"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><=
/mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=3D"on" display=3D"inline"><math x=
mlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>=CE=BB</mi></math></mjx-ass=
istive-mml></mjx-container>, whereas weight averaging does not include such=
 a parameter and is therefore more restrictive. In this sense, would it be =
more appropriate to consider the exponential moving average (EMA), which in=
troduces a comparable degree of flexibility, to make the comparison more in=
tuitive?</p>
</li>
